 msgid "For experts, for projectors and displays without HDR. Where Hades maps an HDR10 video itself (to SDR, or with \"HDR10 and HDR10+ adapted to the display\"), it maps it by the brightness of the whole film, which the studio wrote once. With this on, Hades measures each scene as it plays and maps it by the brightest and the average light of that scene, as it does for Dolby Vision: a dark scene is no longer squeezed as much as the brightest one of the film, so it keeps more of its highlights. Within a scene the mapping changes only slowly, so the picture does not pump. This changes the picture. HDR10+ videos keep their own scene data. Off by default. Applies from the next video."
